Sure. Here's how I'd explain it in an interview:

> The two-pointer approach uses one pointer (`left`) starting from the beginning of the array and another pointer (`right`) starting from the end. 
In each iteration, the `left` pointer moves forward until it finds a `1` that should be on the right, while the `right` pointer moves backward until
it finds a `0` that should be on the left. Once both pointers stop, those two misplaced elements are swapped. The process repeats until the pointers meet or cross. 
Although there are multiple `while` loops, each pointer only moves in one direction and never revisits an index, so every element is examined at most once.
This is why the overall time complexity is **O(n)** and the space complexity is **O(1)**.
